      "hn_id": "50395874",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50395874",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Instructing coding agents to follow formal verification techniques often degrades their output instead of improving it.\n\nDan Luu evaluated frontier models on implementing complex protocols like the Zstd and IMAP specifications from raw RFCs. On earlier iterations, forcing agents to use test-driven development, property testing, or Lean resulted in caricatures of those techniques. The agents generated superficial unit tests or aimless fuzzing inputs that covered the same trivial paths, costing more money while reducing overall correctness compared to unconstrained generation.\n\nWith newer reasoning models, capability jumps dramatically saturate these benchmarks. Implementing the Zstd specification dropped in cost from 3.50 dollars to 1.00 dollar while test correctness surged from 63 percent to over 99 percent. More importantly, when asked to fuzz, the newer model constructed structured inputs that exercised non-trivial edge cases rather than spitting out garbage data.\n\nPrescribing engineering dogmas to an autonomous agent is counterproductive until the model possesses the underlying reasoning capacity to execute them properly."
    },
    {
      "title": "Achieving sample-perfect audio and video synchronization with WebCodecs",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://mediabunny.dev/blog/project-sync",
      "date": "2026-11-06",
      "authors": [
        "Vanilagy"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand the subtle failure modes of audio-video synchronization across media pipelines. You will learn how human perceptual limits mask accumulated timing drift and how to diagnose sync errors using WebCodecs.",
      "hn_id": "50391328",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50391328",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Audio-to-video synchronization looks deceptively simple until you chain multiple transcode operations together. In human perception, an audio delay under 125 milliseconds often passes unnoticed, whereas audio leading video becomes jarring at just 45 milliseconds. However, minor timestamp discrepancies that appear harmless in isolated playback accumulate aggressively across encoding pipelines.\n\nBuilding on top of modern primitives like WebCodecs exposes subtle edge cases in timestamp presentation and duration rounding. When media is transcoded repeatedly, integer rounding errors in sample rate conversions and packet timestamps gradually drift apart. Without explicit sample-level drift compensation and strict timestamp recalculation, a pipeline that looks perfectly aligned on the first pass drifts severely out of sync by the twentieth iteration.\n\nAchieving deterministic media processing requires treating audio clocks and video presentation timestamps as coupled state rather than independent streams. Engineers working on audio, video, or real-time streaming infrastructure must account for underlying frame duration truncations rather than trusting container-level metadata at face value.\n\nPrecise synchronization in distributed or client-side media pipelines is never automatic, it is engineered sample by sample."
    },
    {
