      "content": "Automating design rule checking (DRC) in chip layout presents a familiar verification dilemma: checking a geometry constraint takes seconds, but resolving thousands of interconnected violations requires manual human engineering.\n\nFlexcompute demonstrated that autonomous agents can clear these bottlenecks by coupling layout parsers like KLayout with iterative decision models. Instead of treating layout fixes as pure code generation, the agent operates in an active feedback loop, proposing geometric modifications, validating rule clearance, and executing multi-physics simulations to verify that optical performance remains intact.\n\nThis pattern highlights the true strength of modern agentic workflows. When an agent operates against deterministic verification tools that provide immediate ground truth, it can systematically solve combinatorial spatial problems that previously required days of manual layout tuning."
    },
    {
      "title": "Turning the HEIC decoder into a SHA-256 computer",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.irisc2.com/blog/heic-hashquine",
      "date": "2026-11-06",
      "authors": [
        "IRIS C2 Research"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how video decoding mechanisms like motion vectors and inter-prediction can perform arbitrary computation. You will learn how the authors engineered a compiler targeting the HEVC decode process to create a self-computing hashquine.",
      "hn_id": "50393482",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50393482",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Standard video codecs are far more powerful than simple compression pipelines. By manipulating prediction modes, motion vectors, and macroblock dependencies, the authors turned Apple stock ImageIO HEIC decoder into a full execution engine that calculates its own SHA-256 checksum.\n\nIn HEVC, decoding is not just unpacking pixels; it is an arithmetic pipeline of intra- and inter-frame predictions. The project built a custom compiler that maps SHA-256 message schedules and 64 compression rounds into 564 inter-predicted frames across a 536 MB sequence. When standard decoders process the file, the final rendered frame spells out the exact digest of the container itself.\n\nTreating fixed-function media pipelines as virtual machines exposes the wild computational surface area hiding inside legacy system libraries."
    },
    {
