      "url": "https://www.stet.sh/blog/cost-per-successful-task",
      "date": "2026-11-06",
      "authors": [
        "Stet"
      ],
      "why_read": "Understand why the popular cost per successful task metric can severely distort coding agent benchmarks by rewarding fragile models over reliable workhorses.",
      "hn_id": "50394927",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50394927",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Optimizing coding agents for cost per successful task creates an unexpected failure mode: it systematically rewards the worst models on your benchmark.\n\nWhen calculating cost per success as total spend divided by successful completions, a weak model that attempts only trivial tasks and gives up immediately on harder problems can finish with tiny token usage. On one internal 24-task suite, GPT-6 Luna Low captured the top efficiency spot at twenty-six cents per PR, despite losing every head-to-head match against stronger models. Meanwhile, capable workhorses that tackled complex multi-file refactors appeared ten times more expensive because they actually spent tokens attempting tough problems.\n\nThis inversion happens because unweighted success division fails to account for task difficulty and early-exit penalties. If a benchmark allows agents to fail cheaply without completing the workload, low-capacity models will always look more cost-effective than reliable models that finish the job.\n\nBefore picking a model based on aggregate cost metrics, make sure your benchmark penalizes incomplete tasks and normalizes cost against problem complexity."
