      "content": "Postgres queues built with FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED are popular because they avoid external dependencies like Redis or RabbitMQ, but many engineers do not know where the design actually fails. A recent benchmark push reveals the exact breaking points under high concurrency.\n\nOn a single dedicated CPU core, an unpooled queue hit saturation at roughly 375 jobs per second across 64 concurrent workers. Surprisingly, the database ran out of processor cycles due to lock management and transaction bookkeeping long before running out of default connection slots.\n\nIntroducing a shared pool of 20 connections completely changed the profile. With connection pooling absorbing the context switching overhead, the single-core setup scaled to 128 workers handling over 650 jobs per second before bottlenecking.\n\nBefore abandoning relational databases for a dedicated message broker, measure whether connection churn or CPU lock management is your true bottleneck."
