      "image": null,
      "content": "Vector indexes in production frequently hit memory limits long before they hit compute bottlenecks. Running dense vector representations at scale across billions of embeddings forces teams to choose between expensive high-RAM clusters or severe recall degradation from aggressive quantization.\n\nTieredQ introduces a vector quantization approach called variance-aware bit allocation. Instead of applying a uniform bit budget across every dimension, it rotates vectors into a PCA basis and greedily assigns between 0 and 12 bits per principal component based on variance contribution. High-variance dimensions receive higher fidelity, while low-signal dimensions are heavily compressed.\n\nIn low-budget regimes around 1 to 4 bits per dimension, this greedy allocation strategy delivers equivalent recall while reducing RAM and disk footprint by 36 to 52 percent compared to RaBitQ and TurboQuant. It integrates directly into standard IVF and HNSW vector database indices.\n\nSmart bit allocation across principal components proves that you do not need uniform precision across all embedding dimensions to maintain search accuracy."
