      "date": "2026-11-06",
      "authors": [
        "Lawrence Abrams"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how a defensive timing-attack mitigation in OpenSSH combined with musl's crypt error return value to create a critical authentication bypass. You will gain a clear mechanistic mental model of why sentinel error values can produce catastrophic edge-case failures.",
      "hn_id": "50388083",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50388083",
      "image": null,
      "content": "A subtle vulnerability sitting at the intersection of OpenSSH and the musl C library highlights how defensive code can create disastrous failure modes. Systems like Alpine Linux often disable password authentication on service accounts by setting an asterisk in the shadow file. Because an asterisk is not a valid hash format, authentication should fail under all circumstances.\n\nBack in 2016, OpenSSH introduced a patch to mitigate username enumeration side-channel attacks. To make authentication response times uniform across existing and non-existent users, OpenSSH runs password hashing even when an account has no valid hash. The trouble begins with how musl handles hashing errors. When a submitted password exceeds 256 bytes, musl crypt fails and explicitly returns an asterisk character.\n\nOpenSSH checks the returned string against the stored shadow entry without realizing musl returned an error code rather than an authentic salt-derived hash. The two match, and OpenSSH grants shell access to what was supposed to be a disabled account. Defenses against side-channel leaks must account for C library error semantics, especially in minimal container runtimes."
    },
