      "image": null,
      "content": "Building autonomous multi-step research systems exposes a common fallacy in applied AI: treating agent failures as model shortcomings rather than architectural bugs. When an agent searches, browses, and synthesizes long-form reports across dozens of sources, raw model intelligence is rarely the primary failure mode.\n\nThe real failure mode is orchestrating state and context boundaries. Pushing control flow entirely into model prompts creates fragility and compounding hallucinations. Reliable deep research systems rely on deterministic code for document fetching, token filtering, citation graph validation, and routing, reserving LLM generation exclusively for discrete synthesis steps.\n\nClean separation of concerns between code-level orchestration and model-level reasoning is mandatory. Treating your agent harness as a distributed workflow engine rather than a chat loop yields vastly more predictable production outcomes."
