      "authors": [
        "phirdev"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to learn how parking GPU memory into host RAM and enabling transparent huge pages drastically cuts model swap times. You will understand how to switch between language models on a single GPU without losing prompt caches.",
      "hn_id": "50399642",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50399642",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Running multiple large language models on a single GPU usually forces an unpleasant compromise between cold-start latency and hardware cost. Swapping one GPU-saturating model for another typically takes upwards of ten seconds in llama.cpp, destroying the prompt KV cache and introducing unacceptable delays for interactive applications.\n\nParking inactive model weights in host system memory rather than terminating the process changes this dynamic completely. A lightweight wrapper called llama-sleep holds the model process alive while transferring its VRAM footprint to system RAM, cutting model swap times to 2.1 seconds while preserving warm KV caches intact.\n\nThe remaining bottleneck is not raw PCIe bandwidth, but virtual memory page allocation overhead during host-to-device transfers. Enabling multi-size transparent huge pages at the Linux kernel level resolves memory allocation churn, dropping total swap latency to 1.7 seconds across consumer-grade hardware.\n\nHigh-throughput multi-model inference does not always require multi-GPU clusters when operating system primitives can shoulder the memory management burden."
