        "Farid Zakaria"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how modern build tools like Nix, Bazel, and buck2 optimize build caching through the intensional model. You will learn the mechanistic difference between input-addressed and content-addressed derivations.",
      "hn_id": "50399047",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50399047",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Nix, Bazel, and Buck2 look drastically different from the outside, but they share the exact same underlying design: the intensional model.\n\nMost build systems operate extensionally, meaning they identify build artifacts by the inputs and commands that created them. If a comment in a header file changes, an extensional system invalidates every downstream target regardless of whether the compiled output actually changed.\n\nThe intensional model flips this logic by making derivations content-addressed. Build actions reference inputs and produce outputs indexed strictly by content hashes. When an upstream change leaves the intermediate object code unchanged, the engine detects that the resulting hash matches the cache and halts redundant recompilation.\n\nBazel and Buck2 implement this behavior natively in their action execution graphs, while Nix has spent years standardizing content-addressed derivations. Recognizing this unified model clarifies how hermetic builds achieve massive efficiency in large monorepos.\n\nUnderstanding this design will change how you evaluate caching layers and reproducible build infrastructure."
